'''Johann Baptist Mayrhofer''' (22 October<ref>[[Michael Lorenz (musicologist)|Michael Lorenz]]: [http://michaelorenz.blogspot.com/2012/09/johann-mayrhofers-real-date-of-birth.html "Johann Mayrhofer's Real Date of Birth"], michaelorenz.blogspot.com</ref> 1787{{spaced ndash}}5 February 1836) was an Austrian [[poet]] and [[librettist]]. He is best known for his close friendship with the composer [[Franz Schubert]].

==Biography==
Mayrhofer was born in [[Steyr]], Austria, educated at [[Novitiate]] in [[St. Florian's Priory]], [[Upper Austria]]. In 1810 he began to study [[jurisprudence]] and [[theology]] at the [[University of Vienna]], both of which courses he finished. He worked as a censor at the Zentral-Bücher-Revisions-Amt in Vienna.<ref name=":0">{{Cite book|oclc=10759777|title=Schubert: Die Erinnerungen seiner Freunde |date=1983 |publisher=Breitkopf & Härtel |author=[[Otto Erich Deutsch]]|isbn=3-7651-0186-9 |location=Wiesbaden |pages=9f., 92|language=de}}</ref>

In 1814 he met the young composer [[Franz Schubert]] and his friends ([[Joseph von Spaun]], [[Franz von Schober]]).

From 1818 to 1821 he lived with Schubert in a one-room apartment in a house on Wipplingerstrasse 4.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |title=Wipplingerstraße |url=https://www.geschichtewiki.wien.gv.at/Wipplingerstra%C3%9Fe?uselayout=mobile |access-date=2022-12-18 |website=www.geschichtewiki.wien.gv.at}}</ref>

After Schubert moved out, he wrote the poem "An Franz" for him:
<poem style="margin-left: 2em;">You love me! Deeply I have felt it,
You faithful boy, tender and good;
So steel yourself, beautifully bound,
The noble, youthful courage!
However life presses on,
We hear the kindred sounds. <ref>{{Cite book |last=Stegemann |first=Michael|title="Ich bin zu Ende mit allen Träumen" : Franz Schubert |date=1996 |publisher=Piper |isbn=3-492-03819-0 |location=Munich|pages=227f |oclc=36485726}}</ref><ref name=Todesmusik>{{Cite book|title=Musik-Konzepte – Franz Schubert "Todesmusik" |date=1997 |publisher={{ill|edition text + kritik|de}}|isbn=3-88377-572-X<!-- |volume=no:97-98 -->|location=Munich|page=159|oclc=38134251}}</ref></poem>

Most scholars agree that he was homosexual.<ref name=Todesmusik /> Mayrhofer wrote much lyric poetry and published it in 1824. Forty-seven Schubert songs and two of his operas (''[[Die Freunde von Salamanka]]'' and ''[[Adrast]])'' are based on texts by Mayrhofer.

In 1829 he published his ''Memories of Franz Schubert'' in the journal ''Neues Archiv für Geschichte'',<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mayrhofer |first=Johann |date=1829 |title=Erinnerungen an Franz Schubert, in: Neues Archiv für Geschichte, Staatenkunde, Literatur und Kunst (1829) – Bayerische Staatsbibliothek |url=https://opacplus.bsb-muenchen.de/Vta2/bsb10530619/bsb:7487671?queries=&language=de&c=default |access-date=2022-12-18 |website=opacplus.bsb-muenchen.de}}</ref> through which some remarkable information about Schubert and his circle of friends have been handed down.

Mayrhofer was a hypochondriac all his life: in 1836, during a [[cholera]] epidemic, he committed suicide by jumping from the window of his office in Vienna.<ref>Susan Youens, [https://books.google.com/books?id=b0I6MMBMbFUC&dq=johann+mayrhofer&pg=PA151 ''Schubert's Poets and the making of Lieder''], p. 152</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Deutsch |first=Otto Erich|author-link=Otto Erich Deutsch|title=Schubert: Die Erinnerungen seiner Freunde |date=1983 |publisher=Breitkopf & Härtel |isbn=3-7651-0186-9 |location=Wiesbaden |pages=9f., 76, 92 |language=de|oclc=10759777}}</ref>

